{:edb/db-before-t 1540 :edb/db-after-t 1541 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534853 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:06:29.842Z" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186159752 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159753 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186159788 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159789 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186159788 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159790 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186159788 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159791 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186159788 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159792 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186159788 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159793 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186159788 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441031 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186159788 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441036 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186159788 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441042 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186159788 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441049 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186159788 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441078 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186159788 1078 #edb/ref 17592186441036 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186159800 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159801 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186159800 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159802 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186159800 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159803 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186159800 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159804 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186159800 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159805 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186159800 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441044 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186159800 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441060 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186159800 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441082 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186159800 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441083 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186159800 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441085 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186159800 1078 #edb/ref 17592186441060 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186161703 1071 "Suppose that in a population of children with only one pet, $42\\%$ of children have a dog. A random sample of $n = 40$ children is taken from the population and asked what pet they have.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p}$, the proportion of children in the sample that have a dog? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186161703 1071 "Suppose that in a population of children with only one pet, $42\\%$ of children have a dog. A random sample of $n = 40$ children is taken from the population and asked what pet they have.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ the proportion of children in the sample that have a dog? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186161703 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N$, and we define the sequence of random variables $X_i$ as follows:\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the sample has the characteristic,; 0, otherwise$.\n\nNow, provided that $N \\gg n$, we have that\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\approx B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = \\overline{X} = \\dfrac{X}{n}\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p} ] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p} ] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\np = 42 \\% = 0.42\n$$\n\nand $n = 40$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[p^(^)] &= 42% = 0.42, \\\\\nVar[p^(^)] &= (0.42(1 - 0.42))/(40) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.0061\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186161703 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables $X_i$ as follows:\n![](images/41/412ed703d6c44b1e664317fd734871cfe71d14c944cfa9f9bca84752f635836e.svg)\n\nNow, provided that $N \\gg n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\approx B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}  = \\overline{X} = \\dfrac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p} ] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p} ] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p = 42 \\% = 0.42,$ and $n = 40.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =42%=0.42, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.42(1-0.42)}{40} \\\\  & ≈0.0061\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186161705 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n = 360$ from a population where $35 \\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable $X_i$ be equal to\n![](images/9b/9b117bc5c640d338895493fde26f3c283d7752e1fcb7b0777a3e89a351808f6b.svg)\nwhere $i = 1, \\ldots, 360.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle X = \\sum_{i=1}^{360} X_i\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186161705 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n = 360$ from a population where $35 \\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable $X_i$ be equal to\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the sample has the characteristic; 0, otherwise$\nwhere $i = 1, \\ldots, 360$. If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n$\\displaystyle X = \\sum_{i=1}^{360} X_i$\nfind approximate values of $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[X]$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186161705 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N$, and we define the sequence of random variables $X_i$ as follows:\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the sample has the characteristic; 0, otherwise$\n\nNow, provided that $N \\gg n$, the random variables $X_i$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\nX_i\\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p) \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X$, defined as\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable\n\n$$\nX\\approx B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have\n\n$$\np = 35 \\% = 0.35, \\qquad n = 360\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX\\approx B(360,0.35)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= np \\\\\n&= 360 \\cdot 0.35 \\\\\n&= 126 \\\\\nVar[X] &= np(1 - p) \\\\\n&= 360 \\cdot 0.35 \\cdot (1 - 0.35) \\\\\n&= 81.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186161705 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables $X_i$ as follows:\n![](images/ae/aee44a20433fe178eb22b1af7cf4d7e643b12bf4db69bd4b8a132cf8a59c50b3.svg)\n\nNow, provided that $N \\gg n,$ the random variables $X_i$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\nX_i\\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p) \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X\\approx B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np = 35 \\% = 0.35, \\qquad n = 360.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX\\approx B(360,0.35)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =360\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =126 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =360\\cdot 0.35\\cdot (1-0.35) \\\\  & =81.9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162137 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441113 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162137 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441114 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162137 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441115 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162137 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441116 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162138 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441112 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162138 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441120 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162138 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441122 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162138 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441123 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162139 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441117 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162139 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441118 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162139 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441119 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162139 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441121 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162425 1071 "Suppose we have a population of size $N = 20$, where $55%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable $X_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$X_{i} = {1, ith member of the population has the characteristic; 0, otherwise$\nfor $i = 1, …, 20$. Find $P(X_{2} = 1 \\mid X_{1} = 1)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186162425 1071 "Suppose we have a population of size $N=20,$ where $55%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,20.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1).$" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162425 1074 "We have a population of size $N=20,$ and $p=0.55$ represents the proportion of the population with the characteristic. \n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of population members *with* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nNp=20(0.55)=11,\n$$\n\n- the number of population members *without* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nN(1-p)=20(1-0.55)=9.\n$$\n\nWe select two members of the population at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1),$ the probability that the second member has the characteristic given that the first member also has the characteristic.\n\nIf the first member has the characteristic, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=20-1=19$ members remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $Np-1=11-1=10$ members remaining that have the characteristic.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1)=\\frac{Np-1}{N-1}=\\frac{10}{19}.\n$$" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162431 1071 "Suppose that $45%$ of the voting population plans to vote for candidate $A$ in the upcoming mayoral election. A random sample of size $n = 20$ is taken from the population and asked about their voting preference.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $p^(^)$, the proportion of voters in the sample that plan to vote for candidate $A$? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186162431 1071 "Suppose that $45%$ of the voting population plans to vote for candidate $A$ in the upcoming mayoral election. A random sample of size $n=20$ is taken from the population and asked about their voting preference. \n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of voters in the sample that plan to vote for candidate $A?$ Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162431 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=45%=0.45,$ and $n=20.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =45%=0.45, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.45(1-0.45)}{20} \\\\  & ≈0.0124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162433 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n = 20$ from a population where $55%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable $X_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$X_{i} = {1, ith member of the sample has the characteristic; 0, otherwise$\n\nwhere $i = 1, …, 20$. If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$X = ∑_(i = 1)^(20)X_{i}$\nfind approximate values of $E[X]$ and $Var[X]$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534853 17592186045417 false] [17592186162433 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=20$ from a population where $55%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,20.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186162433 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the prop