ion of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=22%=0.22,$ and $n=25.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =22%=0.22, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.22(1-0.22)}{25} \\\\  & ≈0.0069\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441117 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441100 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441117 1069 "q-146407" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441117 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441117 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=400$ from a population where $32%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,400.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{400}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441117 1072 #uuid "669ea571-4e73-5129-bf0b-c10dd97639ff" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441117 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=32%=0.32,\\,n=400.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(400,0.32)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =400\\cdot 0.32 \\\\  & =128 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =400\\cdot 0.32\\cdot (1-0.32) \\\\  & =87.04\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441118 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441111 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441118 1069 "q-146399" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441118 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441118 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=130$ from a population where $35%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,130.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{130}{X}_{i}.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441118 1072 #uuid "6a0ee3fc-f29f-5b1c-ac8e-c62ce346c425" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441118 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=35%=0.35,\\,n=130.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(130,0.35).\n$$" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441108 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441119 1069 "q-146402" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441119 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=180$ from a population where $48%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,180.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{180}{X}_{i}.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139534853 1759218604541