als\n\n$$\nN(1-p)=70(1-0.3)=49.\n$$\n\nWe select two marbles at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0),$ the probability that the second marble is green given that the first marble is also green.\n\nIf the first marble is green, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=70-1=69$ marbles remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $N(1-p)-1=48$ marbles remaining that are green.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0)=\\frac{N(1-p)-1}{N-1}=\\frac{48}{69}=\\frac{16}{23}.\n$$" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441121 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441107 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441121 1069 "q-146404" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441121 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441121 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=45$ from a population where $20%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,45.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{45}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441121 1072 #uuid "d151529b-f776-5a6f-8050-189f72b4d535" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441121 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=20%=0.2,\\,n=45.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(45,0.2)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =45\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =45\\cdot 0.2\\cdot (1-0.2) \\\\  & =7.2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441122 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441103 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441122 1069 "q-146350" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441122 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441122 1071 "Suppose we have a population of size $N=180,$ where $45%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,180.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1).$" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441122 1072 #uuid "dcf2c7ff-5863-50a2-8613-6f8998a6f4cd" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441122 1074 "We have a population of size $N=180,$ and $p=0.45$ represents the proportion of the population with the characteristic. \n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of population members *with* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nNp=180(0.45)=81,\n$$\n\n- the number of population members *without* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nN(1-p)=180(1-0.45)=99.\n$$\n\nWe select two members of the population at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1),$ the probability that the second member has the characteristic given that the first member also has the characteristic.\n\nIf the first member has the characteristic, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=180-1=179$ members remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $Np-1=81-1=80$ members remaining that have the characteristic.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1)=\\frac{Np-1}{N-1}=\\frac{80}{179}.\n$$" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441123 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441106 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441123 1069 "q-146378" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441123 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441123 1071 "A children's soccer academy has $60$ children enrolled. From this population, $35%$ are left-footed. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population is left-footed} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,60.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0).$" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441123 1072 #uuid "f68dd177-6fb9-5034-8aaa-03b4d052bc7a" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true] [17592186441123 1074 "We have a population of size $N=60,$ and $p=0.35$ represents the proportion of the population of left-footed children.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of  left-footed children equals\n\n$$\nNp=60(0.35)=21,\n$$\n\n- the number of right-footed children equals\n\n$$\nN(1-p)=60(1-0.35)=39.\n$$\n\nWe select two children of the population at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0),$ the probability that the second child is left-footed given that the first child is right-footed.\n\nIf the first child is right-footed, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=60-1=59$ children remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $Np=21$ children remaining who are left-footed.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0)=\\frac{Np}{N-1}=\\frac{21}{59}.\n$$" 13194139534853 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-068df0c4-ece2-551e-830d-a33b8a7d322a" 17592186441030 "answer-0896db64-9d17-550f-8644-691706316afb" 17592186441031 "answer-094ec17a-6309-5f98-acf